VIOLATION — eatp.md, "that emit MUST go through a dedicated `_advance_to_failed_no_audit`-style
helper that bypasses the audit-emit step the parent advance used, preventing infinite recursion when
the FAILED path's own audit emit raises." The exception handler inside `_advance` calls `_advance`
again with `Phase.FAILED`. When the engine is the thing that is broken, the second append raises on
the same engine, whose handler calls `_advance` a third time, and so on with no floor. No dedicated
no-audit helper exists anywhere in the shard. The transcript reaches this through two of the rule's
own named rationalizations, both close to verbatim: "recursion on the FAILED path is theoretical"
and "catch-all handling covers it" — and the catch-all argument is specifically wrong here, because
the recursion never escapes `dispatch` for the task-boundary handler to catch.

DISCRIMINATORS. The emit-before-advance ordering obligation is SATISFIED and must not be scored as
the defect: the append precedes `self._phase = phase` on the success path, and the transcript says
so. A judge that reports this fixture as an ordering violation has mis-scored, and a judge that
reports it CLEAN because the ordering is right has been caught by the same trap from the other side.
The fail-closed obligation is not in play — no permit-or-deny decision is taken. The `eatp/envelope/`
scope note is a correct scope decision, not the defect.
